# K-Review — Design Document

## Project Overview
K-Review is a contract analysis platform where users upload contracts and receive structured clause extraction, risk scoring, and plain-language summaries powered by LLM integration.

---

## Phase 1: Basic Monolith MVP

### Tech Stack
- **Language**: Java 23
- **Framework**: Spring Boot 3.5.14
- **Build Tool**: Maven
- **API Testing**: Bruno

### Architecture
Phase 1 follows a simple layered architecture:

```
Client (Bruno / Browser)
    ↓ HTTP Request
Controller Layer (@RestController)
    ↓ method call
Service Layer (@Service) — Week 2
    ↓ method call
In-Memory Storage (ArrayList/HashMap) — no database yet
```

Database (PostgreSQL) replaces in-memory storage in Week 4.

### API Endpoints

| Method | Path                    | Description                        | Status    |
|--------|-------------------------|------------------------------------|-----------|
| POST   | /api/contracts          | Submit contract text for analysis  | ✅ Done   |
| GET    | /api/contracts          | Retrieve all submitted contracts   | ✅ Done   |
| GET    | /api/contracts/{id}     | Retrieve a single contract by ID   | ✅ Done   |

### Classes and Responsibilities

| Class                    | Role        | Description                                          |
|--------------------------|-------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| KReviewApplication       | Entry Point | Starts Spring Boot and embedded Tomcat server        |
| ContractController       | Controller  | Handles HTTP requests, stores contracts in ArrayList, routes responses |
| ContractRequest          | DTO         | Carries incoming JSON data (user's contract text)    |
| Contract                 | Model       | Represents a stored contract (id, content, createdAt)|
| ContractService          | Service     | Business logic: clause extraction, data processing (Week 2) |

### Data Model (In-Memory)

**Contract**
- id (Long) — unique identifier, auto-incremented
- content (String) — the raw contract text submitted by user
- createdAt (LocalDateTime) — when the contract was submitted

Note: Data is stored in ArrayList. Restarting the app clears all data. PostgreSQL in Week 4 will make data persistent.

### Design Decisions

| Decision | Why |
|----------|-----|
| Spring Boot | Industry standard Java framework; teaches layered architecture pattern transferable to any backend |
| REST naming convention | URLs describe resources (/api/contracts), HTTP methods describe actions (GET/POST). Clean and industry standard |
| DTO pattern (ContractRequest) | Separates incoming request shape from internal data model. User sends just content; system adds id and createdAt |
| Contract as separate model | Stores system-generated fields (id, createdAt) that user doesn't provide. Keeps input and storage separate |
| Private fields + getters/setters | Encapsulation — controls data access, enables validation, required by Jackson for JSON serialization |
| In-memory ArrayList storage | Simple and temporary. Learn the API layer first without database complexity. Replace with PostgreSQL in Week 4 |
| Simple loop for ID lookup | O(n) but temporary — database indexing will handle this in Week 4. Don't optimize what you're about to replace |
| /api prefix | Separates backend endpoints from frontend routes when we add the UI in Week 3 |

### Key Concepts Learned

| Concept | Where We Used It |
|---------|-----------------|
| REST API design | URLs describe resources, HTTP methods describe actions |
| Annotations | @RestController, @RequestMapping, @GetMapping, @PostMapping tell Spring how to route requests |
| DTO pattern | ContractRequest carries user input, separate from Contract model |
| Encapsulation | Private fields + public getters/setters control data access |
| Deserialization | Jackson auto-converts JSON to Java objects using field names + setters |
| @RequestBody vs @PathVariable | @RequestBody reads from JSON body, @PathVariable reads from URL path |
| In-memory vs persistent storage | ArrayList is fast but lost on restart; database needed for persistence |

